   "response_text": "Done and verified. Here's the rundown, boss \ud83d\udc36:\n\n**`invkit/report.py`**\n- `format_report(items, sort_by=\"sku\", descending=False)` \u2014 new keyword-only-ish `descending` (positional-safe, default `False`).\n- Sort now uses `sorted(..., key=..., reverse=descending)`, which reverses ordering for *every* key in `SORT_KEYS` (`sku`, `name`, `quantity`, `price`, `value`) since it's a single generic flag rather than per-key logic. DRY win \u2014 no duplicated lambdas.\n- The `ValueError` guard for unknown sort keys is untouched, so it still fires before any sorting.\n\n**`invkit/cli.py`**\n- Added `--desc` as `action=\"store_true\"` with a help string, threaded into `format_report(..., descending=args.desc)`.\n- Updated the module docstring usage line.\n\n**Checks**\n- `pytest tests -q` \u2192 4 passed (no test files touched, promise).\n- Round-trip property check: for each of the 5 sort keys, descending output is exactly the reverse of ascending.\n- CLI smoke test on a temp CSV: default output and `--sort price` output are byte-identical to the old behavior; `--sort price --desc`, `--sort name --desc`, `--sort value --desc` all reverse correctly.\n\nAssumption worth flagging: ties (e.g. equal prices) fall back to Python's stable-sort input order in both directions \u2014 I didn't add a secondary tiebreak key since that would've changed default output ordering. Also note `low_stock()` has its own ascending sort that `--desc` intentionally does not affect, since the flag is specified to sort the report by `--sort` only.\n\nDONE",
